# Mobile — a speed claim needs a number, and the number needs a home

Three rounds of `apps/mobile` performance work shipped on causes inferred by reading source, and the
reported symptom did not improve. There is now a harness. Use it, and write down what it says.

Durable record: [MOBILE-PERF-BASELINES.md](/docs/development/mobile/MOBILE-PERF-BASELINES.md).

## Read the baseline before measuring

A seeded capture is four to ten minutes of device time plus a reseed, and it needs Metro, the E2E
API, and the right emulator. Before running one, check whether the durable doc already answers the
question. Cite the recorded number instead of re-deriving it.

Run a fresh capture when the code under the number changed, when the question is about a stage no
baseline covers, or when the recorded run is against a materially different device or data set.

## Write the number down where it survives

Plan directories under `.llm/plans/` are deleted when their work lands, so a `FINDINGS.md` is a
working note, not a record. Anything a future change would want to know goes in the durable doc:

- Baseline stage tables, per device, cold and warm kept apart.
- Per-tap counter deltas, not session totals. A session total includes startup and every earlier tap.
- **Rejected changes and why**, with the measured delta. A change that was tried and did not
  explain the stall is the most expensive thing to rediscover.
- Harness defects that made past numbers wrong, so an old artifact is not read as fact.

## A missed gate is a rejected hypothesis, not a worthless change

A gate like "this stage must fall 60%" tests whether the named cause **owns** the cost. Missing it
means the hypothesis is wrong. It does not mean the change did nothing.

When a change misses its gate but moves a user-visible number outside run-to-run variance, revert it
as the plan says, and then **tell the operator the measured delta and let them decide** whether to
keep it on its own merits. Do not discard the result silently, and do not keep it silently either.

## Do not trust a number that is not physically possible

The frame parser reported single frames taking hours, and that was read as a slow app for two
sessions. A measurement that cannot be true is a harness bug:

- Parse `dumpsys gfxinfo framestats` by **column name**. Android 12+ inserts `FrameTimelineVsyncId`
  at index 1, so positional indexing silently subtracts a counter from a timestamp.
- iOS UI-thread frames come from `scroll.uiframes` (native `podverse-perf-probe`); JS-thread frames
  from `scroll.jsframes`. Android also reports `dumpsys gfxinfo` under `frames`. A smoothness claim
  needs one of those numbers.
- Maestro element taps land seconds apart. A scripted "rapid" gesture is sequential unless measured
  otherwise, so claims about superseding an in-flight load come from the manual capture.
- A capture against a fixture with almost no rows cannot exhibit a row-cost bug. Confirm there is
  something to measure before believing a stage is cheap.
- The default volume seed gives every channel the same `localhost:2111` image and no item images.
  `CoverImage` caches by URL, so that seed cannot show unique third-party decode or network cost.
  `PODVERSE_E2E_PERF_REMOTE_IMAGES=1` (only together with the volume flag) assigns the saved
  third-party list in `tools/web/perf-remote-image-urls.json`. Those runs are their own population.
  Do not average them with the localhost-image baseline, and leave the flag unset for regression
  E2E and for the default perf seed.

## Keep the two captures separate

**Seeded** runs (`Pixel_6_Pro_API_33_e2e`, `"iPhone 17 Pro E2E"`, volume seed) are repeatable and are
what a gate compares against. **Manual** runs (`Pixel_6_Pro_API_33`, `"iPhone 17 Pro"`, the
operator's real library, a real hand gesture) are what say whether a change is worth trying.

Never average across captures, devices, or cold and warm passes. When the two disagree in direction,
record the disagreement rather than picking the flattering one.

## Android and iOS do not have the same bottleneck

On the Home chip switch the Pixel is preference-gate bound and the iPhone is paint bound. Measure the
weaker device — a regression shows there first — and do not assume a fix that moved one moved both.

After the shared clear-on-tap, the iPhone stall a finger could feel was expo-image resizing full
covers on the main queue. Off-thread thumbnails at device pixels, tagged with the screen scale,
moved revisit chip visible from about 777 ms to 67 ms (ledger row T5). That is the floor. Do not
start the parked Home follow-ups (kept lists, a playback row store, stable row objects on refresh)
to chase the same-frame targets unless a new manual capture misses that floor, or play or refresh
itself feels heavy. Narrative:
[MOBILE-IOS-CHIP-SWITCH.md](/docs/development/mobile/MOBILE-IOS-CHIP-SWITCH.md). The image rules
are **mobile-image-loading**.

